'''tenet''' (ten′et), ''n.'' [< L. ''tenet'', he holds, 3d pers. sing. pres. ind. of ''tenere'', hold: see ''tenant''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''habitat.'' Cf. also ''tenent''<sup>3</sup>.] Any opinion, principle, dogma, or doctrine which a person, school, or sect holds or maintains as true.

{{smaller block|That all animals of the land are in their kind in the sea, although received as a principle, is a ''tenet'' very questionable.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., iii. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|Though my scheme was not wholly without religion, there was in it no mark of any of the distinguishing ''tenets'' of any particular sect.
{{right|''Franklin'', Autobiog., p. 141.}}}}

{{smaller block|In the ''tenet'' of justification, the believer is himself in contact with the miracle of Christ's atonement, and applies Christ's merits to himself.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Literature and Dogma, ix.}}}}

{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' ''Precept'', ''Dogma'', etc. See ''doctrine''.}}

'''tenfingers''' (ten′fing″gėrz), ''n.'' A starfish with ten arms. Compare ''fivefinger'', 3.

'''tenfold''' (ten′fōld), ''a.'' and ''adv.'' [< ''ten'' + ''-fold.''] Ten times as much or as many.

{{smaller block|I will reward thee
Once for thy spritely comfort, and ''ten-fold''
For thy good valour.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., iv. 7. 15.}}}}

'''ten-forties''' (ten′fôr′tiz), ''n. pl.'' [Short for ''ten-forty bonds'': see def.] The popular name for certain five per cent. bonds issued by the government of the United States in 1864, redeemable at any time after ten years, and payable at the end of forty years.

